#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A break record that quotes an anchor must still be able to find it.

MISE description="Every quoted break anchor resolves exactly once in its target"
"""
# This exists because three records on `main` quoted anchors that no longer
# resolved, and **not one of them was red**. `scripts/break-once.sh` refuses to
# run when its search literal is not exactly-once in the target, so a rotted
# anchor makes the recorded run unreproducible — while the record goes on
# reading like evidence. That is the failure this task turns into a gate.
#
# The rule it enforces is `break-once.sh`'s own precondition, nothing more:
#
#     grep -qF -- "$from" "$file"          # count 0 aborts before any backup
#     assert text.count(old) == 1          # count 2 aborts, backup restored
#
# So the check is `count == 1`, and both failure modes are safe to hit by hand:
# a 0 aborts at the grep before a backup exists, and a 2 aborts at the python
# assert with the backup already restored by `trap EXIT`.
#
# ── four ways a quoted anchor rots, all four found across three branches ────
#
# 1. **Same file.** A record in the file it breaks can make its own anchor a
#    second occurrence: count 2. NOT always, though - a `///` quote carries no
#    indentation while the code does, so an INDENTED search literal stays unique
#    and the record's copy is not a second occurrence. Same-file quoting works
#    where indentation disambiguates, and fails at column 0 or on a fragment.
#    Where it cannot work, describe the search edit - and still quote the
#    REPLACEMENT, which is always safe because `break-once.sh` never searches it.
# 2. **The formatter rewrapped it.** A record quoted a one-line expression;
#    `rustfmt` split it across three when the function's signature widened.
#    Count 0, cross-file, nothing anybody wrote by hand. The formatter owns the
#    line breaks and the record does not.
# 3. **A positional anchor.** A record anchored on a slice of `lib.rs`'s
#    `pub use` list. A later wave inserted one more export into the middle of
#    that slice: count 0. Cross-file and nothing rewrapped, so neither rule
#    above reaches it. What moved was a sibling's presence in an ordered list.
# 4. **A fragment, or a line whose text prefixes another.** The standard advice
#    is "pass the line WITH its indentation", and for these it does not work:
#    a `const` at column 0 has no indentation to add; a mid-line fragment like
#    `pop_front()` is not disambiguated by indentation at all; and a line whose
#    indented text is a PREFIX of another line still counts 2 unless the anchor
#    carries the trailing NEWLINE, which no record thinks to mention. So an
#    anchor must be a **whole line, including indentation, plus the newline when
#    its text prefixes another line.** The abort reads as the harness failing
#    when it is the record that is wrong.
#
# The question that reaches all four: **is the anchor's uniqueness a property of
# the LINE, or of its NEIGHBOURS?** — with the sharpening mode 4 forces, that a
# fragment is not a line at all.
#
# ── THE REMEDY THAT DISSOLVES "THE EDIT SITE SPANS SEVERAL LINES" ─────────────
#
# **`break-once.sh` searches ONE literal and replaces it with anything, so a
# one-line search with a MULTI-LINE replacement is legal — and the replacement is
# never searched, so it cannot affect uniqueness.**
#
# A multi-line edit therefore does NOT need a multi-line anchor. Every row anybody
# has justified with "the edit site spans several lines" was reasoning about the
# SITE when the harness only cares about the SEARCH LITERAL. Three of the five
# conversions on this branch were unlocked by that sentence, and the reason it is
# written here rather than in one row's comment is that its absence is what makes
# the wrong conclusion re-derivable.
#
# So before describing a multi-line edit, ask which single line inside it is
# unique. C14 is the worked example: `.iter()` alone counts 2 and is unusable,
# while the `.filter_map(..)` line one below it counts 1 and anchors the same edit.
#
# ── AND A RECORD IS PART OF THE CORPUS IT DESCRIBES ───────────────────────────
#
# **Writing a record changes the thing the record measures.** The literal you quote
# to document an anchor becomes a second occurrence of it, so the act of explaining
# a break can be what stops the break working.
#
# The asymmetry is worth stating plainly, because it says which edits are risky:
#
#   * REMOVING a quote can only LOWER an occurrence count.
#   * ADDING one can only RAISE it.
#
# **So a requote is the only edit that can turn a resolving anchor into an aborting
# one**, and it does that in the file the author is looking at, while feeling
# careful. Re-reading does not find it; the text looks right. **Count after the
# edit, not after the batch.** And if a count comes back 2, the fix is not "add
# more indentation" — it is that the quote is now byte-identical to something, and
# which occurrence is which has to be seen before choosing.
#
# `break-once.sh` fails safe here: a wrong count aborts at its `grep` or its
# `assert` before any backup is taken, so a bad requote costs a run and not a file.
# But that signal arrives at REPLAY time. The count arrives at edit time.
#
# This is the same insight as the per-tree limitation below, from the other end: a
# record is not an observer of the tree, it is part of it.
#
# ── why this list is written out by hand ────────────────────────────────────
#
# Deriving it would mean parsing prose for "replacing `X` with `Y` in `path`",
# and a heuristic that misses a record checks nothing while reporting success.
# So the list is explicit, exactly as `local.rs`'s
# `no_failure_prefix_is_a_prefix_of_another` spells out its four prefixes and
# says a fifth has to be added by hand.
#
# **The honest limitation, and it is larger than it sounds: this checks the
# records REGISTERED below, not the records that exist.** A sweep of every
# backticked code-shaped span inside a break record, repo-wide, finds them in the
# hundreds. Most are watched output or replacement literals rather than search
# anchors — which is exactly why no extractor can build this list — but the
# consequence stands: a green here means *the registered anchors resolve*, and it
# is not a statement about the health of every record in the tree.
#
# Registration is therefore **opt-in per record**, and the green line says
# "registered" for that reason. When you write a record that quotes a search
# literal, add it. Nothing will notice if you do not, which is the one hole this
# task cannot close from the inside.
#
# **And it checks ANCHORS, never MESSAGES.** A record's watched output is a claim
# about which assertion fires first, and that rots when an assertion is added
# ABOVE the recorded one — the guard stays correct, the test still fails, and only
# comparing the recorded sentence against a fresh run finds it. No count and no
# resolution check can see that. `scripts/replay-fate-breaks.sh` is the tool for
# it, on the set it covers; this task deliberately answers the narrower question
# of whether the break can be STARTED at all.
#
# ── AND THE GUARANTEE IS PER-TREE. IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT A MERGE ───────────────
#
# Every rot mode above is detectable from inside one checkout. **This one is not,
# and no version of this task can be.**
#
# A row can be registered, resolving, replayable and TRUE at branch A's tip while
# branch B rewrites the test the record describes. The literal still resolves at
# exactly one occurrence, so this task stays GREEN on A, on B, and on the merge —
# while the break it certifies no longer reddens anything. Resolution is all the
# checker sees, and the claim died in a file this checkout never had.
#
# Observed, not hypothesised: `C10`'s anchor is a line another branch mutated in an
# experiment and its record is a paragraph that branch rewrote. Its break stopped
# reddening and this task never moved.
#
# **And the registry cannot even REPRESENT the part that goes wrong.** A row is
# `(id, lives_in, target, literal)` — there is **no watched-message field**. So no
# edit to any row can state the claim that died, and no edit to any row can repair
# it. A reader who accepts this limitation and then goes looking for the row change
# that expresses it will not find one, which is why the absence is written down here
# rather than left to be discovered.
#
# So the repair belongs to **the branch that falsifies a row, in the same commit
# that rewrites the file** — otherwise some checkout-able commit has a tree and a
# registry that disagree, and there is no tree from which that is visible. The
# generalisation is worth having: **any registry naming lines in files another
# branch owns is valid at exactly one tree, and the merge is where it silently
# stops being valid.** A green run here is a statement about THIS checkout and
# never about a merge that has not happened.
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys

ROOT = pathlib.Path(
    subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=True,
    ).stdout.strip()
)

# (record id, where the record lives, file the break edits, quoted SEARCH literal)
#
# Only records that QUOTE a search literal belong here. A record that describes its
# edit has nothing to resolve and nothing to rot.
#
# **The three cases where describing is right are NOT the four rot modes above, and
# an earlier version of this line pointed at "the three modes above" as though they
# were the same list.** They are two different lists and they cross:
#
#   * SAME-FILE where indentation cannot separate the record's copy from the code's
#     - rot mode 1, and a describing case only where nothing else separates them;
#   * POSITIONAL - rot mode 3, where re-quoting buys only until the next sibling;
#   * FORMATTER where the edit site spans more than one line - part of rot mode 2,
#     and the only one of the three with no better anchor available.
#
# Mode 4, a fragment, is a rot mode and NOT a describing case: its remedy is to
# widen to the whole line, which is what C12 does. Mode 2 is only a describing case
# when the site has no single line - a one-line anchor inside a wrapped chain is
# quotable, and C13 is quoted for that reason.
#
# **This is a count of ROWS, not of distinct anchors**, and the list holds both
# shapes on purpose: R15 and R16 are two rows sharing one literal because two
# records cite it, while C5 is one row for a literal two records also cite. The loop
# below iterates rows, so `len(ANCHORS)` is rows, and the green line says so.
ANCHORS = [
    ("R15", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "keylink/src/idempotency.rs",
     "Self::Only(named) => named.contains(&method),"),
    ("R16", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "keylink/src/idempotency.rs",
     "Self::Only(named) => named.contains(&method),"),
    ("R17", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "keylink/src/rpc.rs",
     "return Claimed::None;"),
    ("R18", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "keylink/src/rpc.rs",
     "Some(_) | None => KeyState::Fresh,"),
    ("R20", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "keylink/src/idempotency.rs",
     "self.ledger.settle(self.peer, &self.key, slot);"),
    ("R25", "keylink/tests/api.rs", "keylink/src/macros.rs",
     "pub type SystemBuilder = $crate::SystemBuilder<Mail>;"),
    ("R26b", "keylink/tests/api.rs", "keylink/src/rpc.rs",
     "pub fn keyable(mut self, keyable: Keyable) -> Self {"),
    ("R26c", "keylink/tests/api.rs", "keylink/src/rpc.rs",
     "pub fn records(mut self, records: Records) -> Self {"),
    ("R28", "keylink/tests/blobs.rs", "keylink/src/macros.rs",
     "__keylink_granted_id!"),
    # ── from PR #11 (`call-id`). Enumerated by that branch's own gate against
    # `break-once.sh:28`, then re-measured here row by row rather than
    # transcribed: for each, the code site was separated from the record's quote,
    # the indentation read off the code, and the count taken at that exact form.
    #
    # SIX are SAME-FILE and legitimate, which is the narrowed rule in action: the
    # record's quote sits unindented inside a `///` line while the code carries
    # its indentation, so the indented literal is unique and the record's copy is
    # not a second occurrence. Their bare forms count 2-3.
    ("C1", "keylink/src/call.rs", "keylink/src/call.rs",
     "        hasher.update(peer.as_bytes());"),
    ("C2", "keylink/src/call.rs", "keylink/src/call.rs",
     "        hasher.update(key.as_str().as_bytes());"),
    ("C3", "keylink/src/call.rs", "keylink/src/call.rs",
     "        let Some(call) = call else { return };"),
    ("C4", "keylink/src/call.rs", "keylink/src/call.rs",
     "        self.answers.get(&call?)"),
    # Cited from TWO records and registered ONCE, which is the opposite choice from
    # R15/R16 - two rows for one literal, also two records. Both are defensible and
    # the list makes both, so neither is the convention; what the green line counts
    # is ROWS, and that is stated where the count is printed.
    ("C5", "keylink/src/call.rs", "keylink/src/call.rs",
     "            Entry::Occupied(_) => return,"),
    # A FRAGMENT: the line continues `.serialize(serializer)`. Registered as the
    # record quotes it and declared below, rather than widened to the whole line -
    # widening would make this row's literal something no record contains, which the
    # record-quote check rejects. MEASURED, not reasoned: widening C6 gives
    # `FAIL C6: this task's literal is NOT QUOTED by its own record`.
    #
    # That was NOT true while the check searched the whole file, and it was claimed
    # anyway. A review measured it and found the check green on a widened literal,
    # because `lives_in == target` here so the widened CODE line satisfied it. The
    # claim only became true when the check was narrowed to comment spans. Recorded
    # because the decision was right for a reason that did not hold at the time.
    ("C6", "keylink/src/call.rs", "keylink/src/call.rs",
     "        (self.bound, self.iter().collect::<Vec<_>>())"),
    ("C7", "keylink/tests/persist.rs", "keylink/src/call.rs",
     "        call.is_some_and(|call| self.answers.contains_key(&call))"),
    # The one anchor whose record says the TRAILING NEWLINE is load bearing, and
    # it is right: `        self.call` alone counts 2, the text prefixing
    # `        self.call = call;`. Measured here, the INDENTATION is not what
    # disambiguates - `self.call\n` is already unique - but the record's own
    # stated form is kept, since bare-plus-newline would collide the moment
    # another line ends in `self.call` at any indentation.
    ("C8", "keylink/tests/persist.rs", "keylink/src/middleware.rs",
     "        self.call\n"),
    ("C9", "keylink/tests/persist.rs", "keylink/tests/persist.rs",
     "        self.waiting.push((amount, call, deferred));"),
    ("C10", "keylink/tests/persist.rs", "keylink/tests/persist.rs",
     "    parked_over_the_wire(&other, &actor, 1).await;"),
    # ── the two rows that were DESCRIBED and are now quoted. Their old ids were
    # `C-D1` and `C-D2`; renamed because a `-D` suffix in this list would read as
    # "described" and that is what they no longer are.
    #
    # C11 was described on the reasoning that a column-0 declaration has no
    # indentation for the record's quote to lack, so quoting it would make the count
    # 2. That much is true and MEASURED: with the record quoting it, the bare form
    # counts 2. What the old reason got wrong is that it stopped there. The TRAILING
    # NEWLINE disambiguates where indentation cannot, because the record's copy is
    # closed by a backtick and the declaration's by a newline - so the newline form
    # counts 1 and the anchor is quotable after all. This is the second row whose
    # newline is load-bearing, C8 being the first, and the two reach it by different
    # routes: C8's text PREFIXES another line, C11's is a same-file duplicate.
    ("C11", "keylink/src/call.rs", "keylink/src/call.rs",
     "const DIGEST_BYTES: usize = 16;\n"),
